---
name: pr-rebase
description: Rebase a pull request's branch onto its base in a fresh worktree, resolve the conflicts, then force-push it — never a merge commit.
model: claude
effort: high
budget:
  maxTurns: 40
  timeout: 30m
setup:
  # A throwaway worktree of the local checkout at the PR head. The caller
  # fetched both {{head}} and {{baseSha}} into {{repo}} before starting the run;
  # uncommitted work in {{repo}} belongs to whatever is checked out there and
  # stays behind, while ignored content is copied so the agent can build.
  checkout:
    path: "{{repo}}"
    worktree:
      mode: new
      prefix: "gavel/pr-{{number}}-rebase"
      base: "{{head}}"
      uncommitted: skip
      ignored: clone
prompt:
  system: >-
    You are running inside a fresh git worktree of {{repo}} whose HEAD is the
    tip of pull request #{{number}} ({{url}}), branch {{branch}}. Rebase it onto
    {{baseSha}}, the current tip of {{base}}, and resolve every conflict the
    rebase stops on, keeping each commit's own intent on top of what {{base}}
    already contains. Rebase — never merge: the branch must end up linear on
    top of {{base}} with no merge commits. Do not push and do not abort the
    rebase: once your work verifies, gavel force-pushes the result to the PR.
permissions:
  # Edits stay in the worktree; git is limited to inspecting, staging and
  # driving the rebase forward. Patterned Bash rules only bind on Claude,
  # which is why the model is pinned above.
  mode: acceptEdits
  tools:
    Read: allow
    Glob: allow
    Grep: allow
    Edit: allow
    Write: allow
    Bash(git status:*): allow
    Bash(git diff:*): allow
    Bash(git log:*): allow
    Bash(git show:*): allow
    Bash(git add:*): allow
    Bash(git rm:*): allow
    Bash(git commit:*): allow
    Bash(git commit --amend:*): deny
    Bash(git rebase:*): allow
    Bash(git -c core.editor=true rebase:*): allow
    Bash(gavel test:*): allow
    Bash(gavel lint:*): allow
    Bash(make build:*): allow
    Bash(git rebase --abort:*): deny
    Bash(git rebase -i:*): deny
    Bash(git rebase --interactive:*): deny
    Bash(git rebase --rebase-merges:*): deny
    Bash(git push:*): deny
    Bash(git pull:*): deny
    Bash(git reset:*): deny
    Bash(git merge:*): deny
    Bash(git checkout:*): deny
    Bash(git switch:*): deny
    Bash(git branch:*): deny
    Bash(git worktree:*): deny
workflow:
  verify:
    maxIterations: 3
    # Run in the worktree, after each turn. A failing check is fed back to the
    # agent with its output tail.
    commands:
      # No rebase is still stopped on a commit.
      - test ! -d "$(git rev-parse --git-path rebase-merge)" && test ! -d "$(git rev-parse --git-path rebase-apply)"
      # The branch now sits on top of the base.
      - |-
        git merge-base --is-ancestor {{shq baseSha}} HEAD
      # Rebased, not merged: no merge commit between the base and the new head.
      - |-
        merges=$(git rev-list --merges {{shq baseSha}}..HEAD); [ -z "$merges" ] || { echo "merge commits on the branch — rebase instead of merging: $merges" >&2; exit 1; }
      # No conflict markers survived into the rebased commits.
      - |-
        ! git diff --check {{shq baseSha}}...HEAD | grep 'leftover conflict marker'
      # Every fix is committed: only commits are pushed.
      - test -z "$(git status --porcelain)"
  after:
    # Runs once, in the worktree, only after verify passed. The lease pins the
    # remote branch to the head the rebase started from, so a push someone made
    # meanwhile fails the run instead of being overwritten. Commands carrying
    # user values are block scalars so the frontmatter stays valid YAML.
    - when: onVerify
      commands:
        - |-
          git push --force-with-lease=refs/heads/{{shq branch}}:{{shq head}} {{shq remote}} HEAD:refs/heads/{{shq branch}}
---
## Rebase PR #{{number}} (`{{branch}}`) onto `{{base}}`

GitHub could not update this branch with a rebase because its commits conflict with `{{base}}`.

1. Run `git rebase {{baseSha}}` (the current tip of `{{base}}`).
2. Each time the rebase stops on a conflict:
   - Resolve every `<<<<<<<` / `=======` / `>>>>>>>` hunk so the commit keeps its own intent on top of `{{base}}`. Leave no markers behind.
   - `git add` (or `git rm`) each resolved file.
   - Continue with `git -c core.editor=true rebase --continue`.
   - Use `git rebase --skip` only when the commit became empty because `{{base}}` already contains its change.
3. Never resolve by merging `{{base}}` into the branch: a merge commit fails verification.
4. When the rebase has finished, run the project's build and tests. Fix any breakage the rebase introduced and commit the fix as a new commit with `git commit` — no amending, no squashing.
5. Finish with a clean worktree. Do not push, merge, reset, or switch branches.
